  "verbalization": "Nilutamide is hypothesized to have a potential role in the treatment of prostate cancer based on its structural resemblance to Enzalutamide and Bicalutamide, both of which are known to inhibit the androgen receptor. This structural similarity suggests that Nilutamide may share similar mechanisms of action, potentially targeting the same biological pathways as these compounds. The evidence is further supported by the fact that Enzalutamide and Bicalutamide are both classified as androgen receptor inhibitors, and Bicalutamide has been shown to treat prostate cancer in clinical settings. The shared structural features and functional roles in inhibiting the androgen receptor imply a possible mechanism of action for Nilutamide in the context of prostate cancer. The evidence from the visible paths highlights a recurring theme of structural similarity and pharmacological activity in compounds that target the androgen receptor, suggesting that Nilutamide may also exhibit similar therapeutic potential.",
